    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct; my apologies for the error. The main direct service between Osaka and Fukuoka is the Sanyo Shinkansen, known locally as the Sangi Express. It does indeed run directly from Shin-Osaka Station to Hakata Station in Fukuoka, providing a streamlined and direct experience for travelers. There are no unwanted transfers, which ideally suits your preference for simplicity.\n\nTo alleviate any concerns regarding navigating Shin-Osaka Station or large stations more broadly:\n\n1. Utilize station maps: Most bullet train stations in Japan are equipped with multiple maps, including those in English. Shin-Osaka Station, in particular, has a large family zone with maps, which will help you navigate the complex station easily.\n\n2. Purchase a JR Pass: If you're traveling frequently within Japan, a JR Pass can be cost-effective, and it can also simplify your travel as these passes often come with discounts for using Shinkansen services.\n\n3. Download train route apps: Apps such as Hyperdia, Sangi Express, or Google Maps can be helpful not only for getting real-time updates but also for providing station-to-station maps.\n\n4. Follow Japanese customs: Keep your belongings close and secure, remain aware of your surroundings, and maintain",
      "source": "target",
